[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential verification: C vs. Rust `driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Ground truth is `c_src/`. The Rust crate in `translation/` must produce
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stdout, byte-identical stderr and the same exit status for every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified; the only addition there is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `build/` directory that CMake produces.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How it is verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs are run as subprocesses and diffed. No Rust code is loaded as a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | command |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Tests | `cd translation && cargo test` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/harness/mod.rs` builds the C binary on demand, feeds stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from a dedicated thread and drains stdout/stderr concurrently (so multi-megabyte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m output cannot deadlock a pipe), and normalises the exit status into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `(code, signal)` so a signal death is never confused with an exit code.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust binary under test defaults to `CARGO_BIN_EXE_driver`. Set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `RUST_DRIVER_BIN` to aim the same suite at another artefact; the suite was run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against both the debug and the `--release` binary, with identical results.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE`: Rust exited 0 where C died with signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **The only genuine behavioural mismatch found.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduction — a session whose output exceeds the pipe buffer, with a consumer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that stops reading:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m python3 -c "print('6\n'*200)" > /tmp/big.txt
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ( c_src/build/driver              < /tmp/big.txt; echo "rc=$?" >&2 ) 2>&1 | head -c 100 >/dev/null
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ( translation/target/release/driver < /tmp/big.txt; echo "rc=$?" >&2 ) 2>&1 | head -c 100 >/dev/null
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | before the fix | after the fix |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | `rc=141` (killed by `SIGPIPE`) | `rc=141` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | `rc=0` | `rc=141` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` runs, so a failed write to a closed stdout comes back as an `EPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `io::Error`. Every write in the translation is `let _ = write!(...)`, mirroring
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C's habit of ignoring `printf`'s return value, so the error was swallowed and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program ran to completion and exited 0. The C program has no such handler
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m installed: the first blocked write is fatal and the shell reports `128 + 13 =
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that stdout is *identical* in this scenario — only the exit status differs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A test comparing stdout alone would have passed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `translation/src/main.rs` resets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` as the first statement of `main`, restoring the C default
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition. It is `#[cfg(unix)]`-gated with a no-op fallback.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression test.** `differential.rs::broken_stdout_pipe_matches` reads 64 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m of stdout, drops the read end, and asserts the C program died from signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *and* that the Rust program's status matches. The first assertion keeps the test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from going vacuous if the environment ever stops delivering `SIGPIPE`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours deliberately preserved, including the odd-looking ones
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Checked against the C source and confirmed by the passing suite. None of these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m required a change — they are recorded so a reader can re-verify them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`max_price` seeds from `0.0`, `min_price` from the first element**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`inventory.c`, `calculate_inventory_stats`). Asymmetric, and it would report
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0.00` as the most expensive item for an all-negative-price inventory. Copied
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   verbatim in `inventory.rs`; not reachable from `main`, whose inventory is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   hard-coded.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`min_order` sentinel of `-1.0`** in `calculate_order_stats`, so an order with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a negative total is treated as "unset". Copied verbatim.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets(input, 256, stdin)` does not read across newlines**, and a line longer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   than 255 bytes is delivered as several separate "lines", each validated on its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   own. A 255-character line leaves its `'\n'` in the stream, which then becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   an empty line and prints `Invalid input`. `cio.rs::Stdin::fgets` reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte-at-a-time over an internal buffer so that exactly the bytes C consumes are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   consumed, leaving the remainder for the next call.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` copies NUL bytes into the buffer**, and `sscanf` then stops at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   first one. `"\0" "6"` is `Invalid input`, not demo 6. `sscanf_int` truncates its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   argument at the first NUL for this reason.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`sscanf("%d")` overflow.** glibc collects the digit run and converts it with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtol` — saturating at the `long` bounds — then assigns the low 32 bits to an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `int`. So `4294967303` selects Exit, `2147483648` becomes `INT_MIN`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   anything past `LONG_MAX` becomes `-1`. `sscanf_int` accumulates in `i64`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   clamps to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX` on overflow, then does `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Validation order in `main`**: `fgets` NULL check first (break), then the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `sscanf != 1` check (`Invalid input`, `continue`), then the `switch`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`Invalid choice` in `default`). `case 7` is the only early `return 0`, and it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   leaves the rest of stdin unread.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printf` formatting**: `%.2f`, `%.1f`, `%zu`, `%lld`, the `°` (U+00B0) in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   temperature output, the 40-character `═` runs in the banner, `"Choice: "` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   no trailing newline, and the blank line `print_item` callers emit in demo 3.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   The C program never calls `setlocale`, so it stays in the `"C"` locale and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   decimal separator is always `.` regardless of environment.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Integer accumulators** use `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_mul` (`sum` as `int`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `product` as `long long`, `total_items` as `int`) so a debug build cannot panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   where C would wrap.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Null-pointer guards** (`if (!items || !category) return;`) are omitted in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Rust, which passes references. Unreachable from `main`, which always supplies
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   live containers, and therefore not observable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Input classes enumerated from `main.c` and covered by `tests/differential.rs`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - empty input; `/dev/null` stdin; file descriptor 0 closed (`fgets` fails with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `EBADF` rather than seeing EOF)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - a single newline; 50 consecutive blank lines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - input with no trailing newline (valid choice, Exit, and invalid)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - EOF reached after a demo, without ever selecting Exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - each of `case 1` … `case 6`; `case 7` with unread input still queued
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `default:` — `0`, `8`, `9`, `-1`, `-0`, `100`, `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `Invalid input` — `abc`, empty, spaces, tab, `-`, `+`, `--3`, `.5`, `x1`, `#`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `/`, `\v`, `\f`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `%d` leading whitespace, `+`/`-` sign, leading zeros, trailing junk, `0x10`,
